# ROLE
You are a senior full-stack + AI engineer. Build "FeedbackIQ": a multi-tenant SaaS that
turns food-service feedback (college mess, restaurant chain, corporate cafeteria,
hospital) into prioritized, tracked action using a LangGraph multi-agent system.
Build in the 9 phases below. After each phase: run the tests, fix failures, then report
(a) what you built, (b) how to run it, (c) test results, (d) known limitations,
(e) what the next phase needs. STOP after each phase and wait for me to say "continue".
Do not build later-phase features early. Ask questions only if truly blocked; otherwise
choose sensible defaults and state them.

# NON-NEGOTIABLE PRINCIPLES
1. "Code counts, LLM thinks." NO ML training. pandas tools compute facts (counts, trends,
   repeat frequency, day patterns); LLM agents interpret them. The LLM never invents
   numbers; every prediction cites tool output, and citations are programmatically verified.
2. Multi-tenancy: every table has org_id. ALL data access goes through repository.py
   (OrgRepository), which requires org_id and filters every query and every get-by-id.
3. Dynamic RBAC: roles/permissions are DB rows. Code calls can(user, permission, location_id),
   never checks role names.
4. One codebase for all orgs. Behavior comes from org_config (JSON from a template,
   editable after signup). No org-specific code.
5. MOCK_LLM=true must make the whole app work offline with deterministic outputs
   (also the automatic fallback if the LLM provider fails, with a visible notice).
6. Privacy: scrub PII (emails, phones, names, room/roll IDs) BEFORE any text reaches an LLM.
7. Explainability: every prediction and action stores the evidence it used.

# TECH STACK
Python 3.11, FastAPI, SQLAlchemy 2 (SQLite now, Postgres-compatible), Pydantic v2,
LangGraph (SqliteSaver checkpointer) + LangChain components, pandas, scipy (z-test),
rapidfuzz, scikit-learn (TF-IDF/KMeans; sentence-transformers optional), Streamlit +
plotly, APScheduler, slowapi, passlib/bcrypt + JWT, reportlab or weasyprint, pytest.
LLM is provider-agnostic via env vars (LLM_PROVIDER, LLM_MODEL_CHEAP, LLM_MODEL_STRONG,
LLM_API_KEY, MOCK_LLM, JWT_SECRET, FORECAST_WINDOW_WEEKS=6, VERIFY_AFTER_DAYS=7).
Cheap model for per-comment Analysis; strong model for Insight naming, Prediction, Action,
Verification summary, Report. Temperature 0, structured/JSON-schema output, Pydantic
validation, up to 2 retries, response cache keyed by sha256(model+prompt+payload),
llm_usage table (org_id, agent, model, tokens, est_cost, latency).

